We need to analyze the problem.

The journey steps:

Step 0: seek selector "#checkin"
Step 1: type "2026-12-04"
Step 2: press Tab -> expects focus moves to #guests but fails (focus remains on #checkin)
Step 3: type "2" expecting still focused on #guests? Actually step 3 asserts focus on #guests again.

Observed: After tab key press, focus didn't move to #guests; instead remained on #checkin. In the observed state, after step 2 they saw focus still on #checkin (the same bbox). So something prevented moving focus away from the input when tab pressed—likely due to some JavaScript handling keydown on the input preventing default or focusing back.

Looking at index.html script: They instantiate Pikaday on the checkin field. Pikaday likely adds a keydown listener on document (via opts.keyboardInput true) that handles arrow keys etc. It may also prevent default for Tab? Let's examine pikaday.js code for _onKeyChange.

From source:

self._onKeyChange = function(e)
{
    e = e || window.event;

    if (self.isVisible()) {

        switch(e.keyCode){
            case 13:
            case 27:
                if (opts.field) {
                    opts.field.blur();
                }
                break;
            case 37:
                self.adjustDate('subtract', 1);
                break;
            case 38:
                self.adjustDate('subtract', 7);
                break;
            case 39:
                self.adjustDate('add', 1);
                break;
            case 40:
                self.adjustDate('add', 7);
                break;
            case 8:
            case 46:
                self.setDate(null);
                break;
        }
    }
};

Note: No handling for Tab (keyCode 9). So it doesn't prevent default. However maybe the date picker UI (the calendar) is visible and captures focus inside it, causing tab to stay within the date picker? But the observed focus after tab is still on the input (#checkin). That suggests that when tab is pressed, focus goes to something else but then immediately returns to the input, perhaps due to blur event causing show() again?

Let's see other listeners: _onInputBlur hides the picker after a timeout unless _c is true (which indicates interacting with selects?). In _onInputBlur:

self._onInputBlur = function()
{
    // IE allows pika div to gain focus; catch blur the input field
    var pEl = document.activeElement;
    do {
        if (hasClass(pEl, 'pika-single')) {
            return;
        }
    }
    while ((pEl = pEl.parentNode));

    if (!self._c) {
        self._b = sto(function() {
            self.hide();
        }, 50);
    }
    self._c = false;
};

So when input loses focus (tab away), blur fires, checks if active element is inside pika-single (the picker). If not, hides after 50ms unless _c true. Meanwhile, maybe the picker is visible and contains focusable elements (like buttons for prev/next month). When tab is pressed, focus goes to the first focusable element inside the picker (maybe the prev button). Then after blur timeout (50ms) the picker hides, causing focus to return to the input? Actually when picker hides, its elements are removed from DOM or display:none, causing focus to go back to whatever previously had focus? Usually when an element loses focus because its container is hidden, focus may go to body or remain? Might cause focus to return to the input because the input still has focus? Not sure.

But observed: after tab, focus still on #checkin (the input). Means that the blur didn't actually cause focus to leave? Or focus left then quickly returned due to some refocus.

Maybe the issue is that the Pikaday instance sets aria-label on the input when bound: opts.field.setAttribute('aria-label', opts.ariaLabel); This occurs in draw() when opts.bound true. In our case, bound is true? Let's see index.html: field: document.getElementById('checkin'); defaultDate set; no explicit bound option; default bound is undefined, then in config: opts.bound = !!(opts.bound !== undefined ? opts.field && opts.bound : opts.field); Since opts.bound undefined, opts.field is truthy, so opts.bound = true. So bound true.

Thus draw() runs and sets aria-label each time. That shouldn't affect focus.

Potential cause: The Pikaday instance might be focusing the input again after showing? Let's look at show():

show: function()
{
    if (!this.isVisible()) {
        this._v = true;
        this.draw();
        removeClass(this.el, 'is-hidden');
        if (this._o.bound) {
            addEvent(document, 'click', this._onClick);
            this.adjustPosition();
